    <bpmn:userTask id="discover" name="Reads Discover, on a phone">
      <bpmn:documentation>A plain arrival on a phone opens on a page in front of the map: the map as one card, then new lists, the most opened places, a Play card offering lists to go round, swiped through one at a time with the reader's own walks first, popular lists and what is new on the map. Open map, a place on a shelf, the Places pill or an answer from the find bar leaves it for the map, and Back returns to it. A link about something — a place, a list, a chip — skips it, and so does a desktop.
when: discover_map
when: discover_list
when: discover_place
when: discover_play
when: discover_play_more
when: discover_all
when: discover_make
ref: assets/discover.js
ref: assets/app.js mountDiscover()
ref: functions/api/discover.js GET
ref: index.html #discover</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>visitor-e2</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>visitor-e3</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:userTask>
    <bpmn:userTask id="browse" name="Browses the map, the chips and the search">
      <bpmn:documentation>A chip that would leave nothing on screen pulls the map back instead. Surprise me picks from what the chips allow. A search of five letters or more can be looked up on Google Maps as well: what Google finds goes on the map as results, and is kept for the next search. The locate button draws the dot the list measures from, and takes a typed place when the device will not say.
when: filter_select
when: search@map
when: find_pick
when: find_word
when: find_submit
when: find_area
when: find_google
when: random_pick
when: cluster_open
when: where_pick
ref: assets/app.js applyFilters()
ref: assets/app.js openWhere()
ref: assets/app.js findLookUp()
ref: functions/api/lookup.js
ref: data/taxonomy.json</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>visitor-e5</bpmn:incoming>
